.TH GP-FORK 1 "2026-10-05" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
gp-fork \- work with a fork of someone else's repository
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gp fork sync
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B gp fork status
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
.br
.B gp fork pr
[\fIgh pr create options\fR ...]
.br
.B gp fork
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A clone of a fork has two remotes:
.I origin
(your fork) and
.I upstream
(the repository it was forked from, the name
.B gh repo fork \-\-clone
gives it; otherwise add it with
.BR "git remote add upstream <url>" ).
Every subcommand needs that upstream remote. The base is origin's default
branch unless
.B \-b
names another.
.SS sync
Catch the fork's base branch up with upstream's, both in your clone and on
origin. It does what
.B git pull upstream <base> && git push origin <base>
does, but from any branch, and it only ever fast-forwards. The local base is
updated wherever it lives: in this worktree, in another worktree, or not
checked out at all. Upstream's tip is then pushed to origin with a plain push,
never forced.
.PP
A fork's base should only follow upstream, so a base with commits of its own
is left as is and reported, here or on origin, and the exit status is 1. So
is a fast-forward that uncommitted changes would overwrite.
.PP
It only touches the base branch. To rebase a feature branch onto the fresh
base, follow it with
.BR "gp sync" ,
which works against origin as usual.
.SS status
Fetch both remotes, then show how the base here and on origin compares with
upstream's (up to date, ahead, behind), and list your branches, here or on
origin, whose names upstream doesn't have. Each shows where it lives (here
only, on origin only, or both) and how many commits it holds that upstream's
base lacks;
.I merged into upstream/<base>
means none, so the branch can go. Changes nothing apart from the fetch.
.SS pr
Open a pull request from the current branch to upstream: runs
.B gh pr create
with
.B \-\-repo
set to upstream,
.B \-\-head
set to
.IR <your-fork's-owner>:<branch> ,
and
.B \-\-base
set to the base unless you pass
.B \-B
or
.B \-\-base
yourself. Plain
.B gh pr create
in a fork can open the pull request against the fork itself, depending on
which repository gh treats as the default.
.PP
The branch is pushed to origin first when origin lacks it or is behind (never
forced). If origin's copy has commits yours doesn't, it stops and asks you to
pull or rebase. It refuses to run on the base branch. Every argument goes to
.B gh pr create
unchanged, so
.BR \-\-title ,
.BR \-\-body ,
.BR \-\-fill ,
.BR \-\-draft
and
.B \-\-web
all work. The owner and repository names are read from the remotes' URLs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fINAME\fR
(sync, status) Use
.I NAME
instead of origin's default branch.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
(sync) Fetch, then print what would happen without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B sync
exits 0 when the base is up to date with upstream, both here and on origin,
and 1 when something was left as is.
.B status
exits 0.
.B pr
exits with
.BR gh 's
status. Any subcommand exits 1 when a guard fails and 2 for a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gp fork sync
Pull upstream's latest base into your clone and push it to your fork.
.TP
.B gp fork sync && gp sync
Then rebase the current feature branch onto it and push.
.TP
.B gp fork status
See what upstream has that you don't, and which of your branches it lacks.
.TP
.B gp fork pr \-\-fill \-\-draft
Push the current branch to your fork and open a draft pull request to upstream.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B pr
needs the GitHub CLI
.RB ( gh ),
signed in to an account that can open pull requests on upstream.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gp-sync (1),
.BR gowork (1),
.BR gh-pr-create (1),
.BR gh-repo-fork (1)
